  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm and humid conditions will continue today, \nwith showers and scattered thunderstorms through this evening.\n\nGOES water vapor imagery shows a southern stream shortwave trough \nacross Pennsylvania early this morning. An axis of PWATS over 1.5 \ninches within a region of strong moisture transport has moved\neast and so has the steady showers. A very moist airmass will \nremain across the forecast area, with dewpoints remain in the \nmid to upper 60s. Patchy fog and stratus are expected through \ndaybreak. \n\nA surface trough is expected to move across the region this morning, \nand a few showers can't be ruled out due to weak low-level \nconvergence. A strong northern stream trough will move into the \nGreat Lakes region today with an associated cold front approaching \nthe forecast area before passing through this evening. Instability \nwill develop across most of the region today. A southwest wind will \nlikely shadow areas downwind of the Lakes, keeping conditions mostly \ndry there. Showers and scattered thunderstorms are expected to \ndevelop along and inland from lakebreeze. Forecast soundings \nsuggest increasing mid-level winds and steepening mid-level lapse \nrates ahead of the cold front. Isolated severe thunderstorms are \npossible with damaging winds and large hail as the primary hazards. \nThe main area of concern is the inland areas of the western Southern \nTier extending to the Finger Lakes region. Most showers and \nthunderstorms will be progressive, though brief heavy downpours \nare possible. Some HREF members show a line of heavy showers \nand thunderstorms developing across the Niagara Pennsiula to the\nNiagara Frontier this afternoon. A narrow region of localized \nheavy rain and an isolated severe thunderstorm are possible. \n\nThe cold front and the leading edge of the trough will move across \nthe region this evening. A ribbon of mid-level moisture will \nmaintain chances for showers. Cold air advection and drier air will \nfilter into the region tonight, with 850mb temperatures tumbling \nthrough Monday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Brief cooldown Monday through early Tuesday morning, \nthen trending warmer and unsettled through the middle of the week.\n\nDrier and much cooler weather will settle across the region Monday \nthrough Monday night. Northwest flow and 850mb temperatures \nbottoming out to as low as +3C will result in below normal \ntemperatures. High temperatures will range from the mid to upper \n60s, to the lower 60s across the higher terrain. Surface high \npressure will move across the region Monday night. Temperatures will \nfall into the 40s, with the potential for locations across the \nSouthern Tier valleys and Tug Hill region to reach the upper 30s. \nPatchy frost can't be ruled out across portions of Lewis county \nMonday night. \n\nAn upper level ridge will build briefly across the Great Lakes \nregion Monday night through Tuesday before settling south of the \nregion through mid-week. Warmer weather will return to the region as \na frontal boundary with increasing moisture stalls near the forecast \narea. This may bring periods of showers and thunderstorms through \nThursday.",
